    <exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      One specific form of the Romanovski differential equation is
      <md>
        <mrow>(1+x^2)y'' - n(n-1) y = 0.</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find two linearly independent solutions to this equation by using our
        power series method.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Explain why there is a polynomial solution if <m>n = m</m>, a
        non-negative integer.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the polynomial solutions when <m>n=m=2,3,4,5</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Solve the wave equation
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{\partial^2u}{\partial t^2} = 16\nabla^2 u</mrow>
      </md>
      for the circular membrane of radius 5.  
      The membrane is clamped on the
      boundary of the disk so that <m>{u = 0}</m> on the boundary.  The initial
      velocity is zero and the initial position is
      <md>
        <mrow>u(r,\theta, 0) = 2J_1(z_{1,4}r/5)\sin(\theta) + 6J_3(z_{3,1}r/5)\sin(3\theta)-J_4(z_{4,4}r/5)\cos(4\theta)</mrow>
      </md>
      where <m>z_{n,m}</m> is the <m>m^{th}</m> zero of <m>J_n(x)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      You should be able to obtain an explicit solution given this information.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the general solution to Laplace's equation
      <m>{\nabla^2 u = 0}</m> in a cylinder of radius 3 and height 2 assuming
      that <m>{u=0}</m> on the <q>curvy</q> side and the bottom of the cylinder,
      but not necessarily the top.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<p>
    (Continued on the next page.)
</p>
</page>
<page>
<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose a friend calls to say that she
      bought a non-square, rectangular drum at a flea market.  When she hits
      it for you, you detect that the lowest frequency is 40 Hz and the
      second lowest frequency is 60 Hz.
      After thinking about it for a few
      minutes, you tell her the ratio of the longer side to the shorter side
      of the drum.  What is this ratio and how did you find it?
    </p>
    <p>
      Remember that the vibrations of the drum are determined
      by the wave equation
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} = v^2\nabla^2 u,</mrow>
      </md>
      where the boundary of the drum is clamped, a frequency of one Hz is one
      cycle per second, and the function <m>\cos(\omega t)</m> has a frequency
      of <m>\omega/2\pi</m> Hz when <m>t</m> is measured in seconds.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

